<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese-nbsp">How to Make Egg Bites with Cottage Cheese&nbsp;</h2>



<p>Aside from the simple ingredients, these cottage cheese cups come together in just a few simple steps, requiring only a large bowl and a whisk. Here&rsquo;s exactly how I make them:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites.jpg" alt="A casserole dish filled with water in an oven." class="wp-image-83963" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-and-cottage-cheese-bites.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Prep the oven: </strong>Set your oven to 325&deg;F (162&deg;C). Place a 9&times;13 dish with 2 cups of hot tap water on the bottom rack.&nbsp;</p>



<p>I know it sounds like an extra step, but the steam created by the water as the egg bites bake is what keeps them soft and custardy. It also helps the eggs bake evenly without turning rubbery or completely deflating as they cool, similar to the Starbucks version made with a sous vide machine.</p>



<p>That said, you can skip this step if you prefer. The egg bites will still turn out delicious, just a bit firmer in texture.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese.jpg" alt="A muffin tin with parchement liners." class="wp-image-83964" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-cups-with-cottage-cheese.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Grease or line your muffin pan:</strong> Most egg bite recipes online use a silicone muffin pan to bake the bites. Sadly, I do not own one. If you have it, I would still recommend greasing it before pouring in the egg mixture.</p>



<p>If you are like me, and only have a regular metal 12-cup muffin tin, then I highly recommend lining them with <a href="https://amzn.to/4mHzRDq" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">parchment liners</a>. It is truly the <strong><em>foolproof</em> solution</strong> to prevent the egg bites from sticking to the pan. I tried using paper liners, and while the egg cups did not stick to the pan, it was hard to remove the paper from the egg bites in one piece.</p>



<p>Otherwise, take your time greasing/buttering the muffin tins well, covering all edges and even the top, as eggs always stick to metal if not greased properly.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese.jpg" alt="Eggs whisked in a bowl with spinach." class="wp-image-83965" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Egg-bites-with-cottage-cheese.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Whisk the eggs: </strong>Crack the eggs into a large bowl along with salt (if using) and pepper until they are fully combined. Try not to overmix as they can trap too much air, causing them to puff up in the oven and then deflate as they cool. A little deflation is normal, but whisking just until smooth helps keep their shape.</p>



<p><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Add the remaining ingredients: </strong>Stir in the cottage cheese and shredded cheddar until everything is well combined. Fold in the roughly chopped baby spinach.</p>



<p>It is easier to switch from a whisk to a spatula when folding.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Additionally, while I listed only 2 cups of baby spinach in the recipe card below, you can use up to 3 cups if you prefer to up the nutritional value of these cottage cheese egg cups. Just make sure they are chopped a bit so you don&rsquo;t end up with big chunks of spinach in your egg muffins.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites.jpg" alt="Muffin tin with egg and cottage cheese bites before and after they are baked in the oven." class="wp-image-83967" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/High-protein-cottage-cheese-egg-bites.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Fill the muffin cups: </strong>Scoop about &frac14; cup of the mixture into each muffin well. If you are using a standard 12-cup muffin tin, fill it closer to the top. If you are using a different size, divide the batter evenly among the cups, keeping in mind that the baking time may vary.</p>



<p><strong>Step 6 &ndash; Bake</strong> at 325&deg;F (162&deg;C) for 35 to 40 minutes, with the muffin pan positioned directly above the water dish.</p>



<p>You&rsquo;ll know they are done when the tops look set, and a toothpick inserted in the center comes out clean. The lower temperature, combined with steam, is the secret to that custardy, Starbucks-style texture.</p>



<p><strong>Step 7 &ndash; Let them rest: </strong>When they first come out of the oven, they will appear puffy, but they will deflate as they cool. I like to let them cool for 10 minutes before removing them from the muffin tin, so they are not too hot.&nbsp; </p>



<p>You can enjoy them warm or let them cool completely if you are making them as part of your weekly meal prep.</p>
</div>



